  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to repeal sections 3, 4, 5 and 6 of chapter 160 of the laws of 2013,\nrelating to authorizing the New York state canal corporation to abandon\ncertain canal lands and authorizing the commissioner of general services to\nsell and convey the right, title and interest of the people of the state of\nNew York, in and to certain lands of the New York state canal corporation\nlocated in the city of Syracuse, county of Onondaga, relating to the sale and\nconveyance of such land",
    "basePrintNo": "S6963",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6963-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Rpld §§3, 4, 5 & 6, Chap 160 of 2013",
    "lawSection": "Canal",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6963\n\nSPONSOR: MANNION\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to repeal sections 3, 4, 5 and 6 of chapter 160 of the laws of\n2013, relating to authorizing the New York state canal corporation to\nabandon certain canal lands and authorizing the commissioner of general\nservices to sell and convey the right, title and interest of the people\nof the state of New York, in and to certain lands of the New York state\ncanal corporation located in the city of Syracuse, county of Onondaga,\nrelating to the sale and conveyance of such land\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill repeals part of Chapter 160 of the Laws of 2013 to allow the\nOffice of General Services to transfer land in the Syracuse Inner Harbor\nto the City of Syracuse.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Repeals sections 3, 4, 5, and 6 of Chapter 160 of the Laws of\n2013.\n\nSection 2: Establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 2013, legislation was passed to authorize the NYS Canal Corporation\nto abandon and sell certain lands in the Syracuse Inner Harbor for\nprivate development. The law authorized the Office of General Services\nCOGS) to sell that land. However, the sale and the development never\ncommenced. Now the City of Syracuse is seeking control over that land to\npreserve it for public use as parkland. In order for OGS to complete\nthat transaction, parts of the 2013 statute need to be repealed.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
